# Student Event Sync Batch — Functional Design


## 1. Overview

A scheduled batch job reads new rows from `student_event`, fetches the matching `student` record, and processes it in strict order. It resumes safely after any failure or crash without re-reading records already done.

**Scope**

- Runs on a scheduler (for example every 12 hours); only one instance runs at a time.
- Reads events in batches of up to 500 (configurable) and loops until no events remain.
- Tracks every run and every batch iteration for reporting and troubleshooting.

**Key design principles**

1. **Order by time.** `event_id` is a UUID and cannot be sorted, so events are ordered by `event_last_modified_ts`, with `event_id` as the tie-breaker.
2. **Checkpoint per iteration.** Each iteration saves its checkpoint only after its work is committed. A crash loses at most one in-flight batch.
3. **Resume from the last successful iteration.** Failed or crashed iterations are ignored when finding the resume point.
4. **Status is the lock.** A new run starts only if the latest run is `SUCCESS` or `FAILED` (or a stuck run is stale).
5. **Reprocessing is acceptable, skipping is not.** Redoing one batch is safe; missing an event or breaking order is not.

## 2. Tables

Four tables: two existing source tables and two new tracking tables. The resume point is read from `batch_iteration` only.

### 2.1 student\_event (existing, source)

| Column | Type | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| event\_id | UUID | Unique, not sortable; used only as tie-breaker |
| student\_id | VARCHAR / BIGINT | Student that changed |
| event\_last\_modified\_ts | TIMESTAMP | Ordering column; should be DB-generated |

Recommended index: `(event_last_modified_ts, event_id)`.

### 2.2 student (existing, target)

| Column | Type | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| student\_id | PK | Looked up per event |
| ...student fields... |  | Fetched and processed |
| active | BOOLEAN | Set to `false` when the student is not found |

Only this batch writes to `student`.

### 2.3 batch\_scheduler (new, one row per run)

| Column | Type | Notes | Example |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| run\_id | BIGINT, PK, auto-increment | Latest run = `MAX(run_id)` | 1024 |
| status | VARCHAR | `INITIATED`, `PROCESSING`, `SUCCESS`, `FAILED` | SUCCESS |
| run\_start\_ts | TIMESTAMP | When the scheduler fired | 2026-10-01 08:00:00 |
| run\_end\_ts | TIMESTAMP | When the run finished (null while running or after a hard crash) | 2026-10-01 08:04:12 |
| total\_processed | INT | Records processed in this run | 1500 |
| event\_from\_last\_modified\_ts | TIMESTAMP | Earliest event last-modified time this run started from | 2026-09-30 14:22:10 |
| event\_to\_last\_modified\_ts | TIMESTAMP | Latest event last-modified time this run reached | 2026-10-01 07:59:58 |
| failed\_ts | TIMESTAMP | Set when status = `FAILED` | null (set only on failure) |
| failed\_reason | VARCHAR | Error message or code | null |

### 2.4 batch\_iteration (new, one row per batch of up to 500)

| Column | Type | Notes | Example |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| iteration\_id | BIGINT, PK, auto-increment |  | 50213 |
| run\_id | FK to batch\_scheduler | Groups iterations by run | 1024 |
| iteration\_no | INT | 1, 2, 3 ... within the run | 2 |
| status | VARCHAR | `PROCESSING`, `SUCCESS`, `FAILED` | SUCCESS |
| iteration\_start\_ts / iteration\_end\_ts | TIMESTAMP | Whole iteration (fetch + process) | 2026-10-01 08:01:30 / 2026-10-01 08:02:45 |
| fetched\_count | INT | Rows fetched (max 500) | 500 |
| processed\_count | INT | Distinct students processed after dedup | 462 (38 duplicate student\_ids collapsed) |
| event\_from\_last\_modified\_ts / event\_from\_id | TIMESTAMP / UUID | Checkpoint this iteration started from | 2026-09-30 18:40:05 / 7c9e6679-7425-40de-944b-e07fc1f90ae7 |
| event\_to\_last\_modified\_ts / event\_to\_id | TIMESTAMP / UUID | Last event processed = new checkpoint | 2026-10-01 03:12:47 / 3f2504e0-4f89-11d3-9a0c-0305e82c3301 |
| failed\_reason | VARCHAR | Set when status = `FAILED` | null (e.g. "SQLException: connection reset" when FAILED) |

The `from_*` and `to_*` columns give the event last-modified time range each iteration covered. The `to_*` of the latest `SUCCESS` iteration is the resume point, and rolls up to the run's `from_event_last_modified_ts` / `to_event_last_modified_ts` so you can see the full window a whole run covered.

## 3. End-to-end flow

&#91;embedded content: batch run flow · init, iteration loop, success and fail exits\]

A run loops over iterations until a fetch returns fewer than 500 events; any error ends the run as `FAILED`, and the next run resumes from the last saved checkpoint.

## 4. Use case 1 — Init (scheduler starts)

The scheduler fires and decides whether a new run may start.

**Steps**

1. Read the latest row: `SELECT * FROM batch_scheduler ORDER BY run_id DESC LIMIT 1`.
2. Apply the start rule:

| Latest run status | Action |
| --- | --- |
| No row (first ever run) | Start; checkpoint = beginning (or a configured start time) |
| `SUCCESS` | Start a new run |
| `FAILED` | Start a new run (resumes from last successful iteration) |
| `INITIATED` / `PROCESSING`, not stale | Exit; another run is active |
| `INITIATED` / `PROCESSING`, older than stale timeout | Mark it `FAILED`, reason `STALE_RUN_TIMEOUT`, then start |

3. Insert a new `batch_scheduler` row: status `INITIATED`, `run_start_ts = now`.
4. Read the resume checkpoint from the latest `SUCCESS` iteration (section 8, query B).
5. Count pending events after the checkpoint.
6. If the count is 0: set status `SUCCESS`, `run_end_ts = now`, `total_processed = 0`, and exit.
7. Otherwise set status `PROCESSING` and start the iteration loop (use case 2).

**What the statuses tell you when stuck**

- Stuck on `INITIATED`: died before any batch started (for example DB connection failed).
- Stuck on `PROCESSING`: died in the middle of a batch.

## 5. Use case 2 — Success (iteration loop)

Each iteration processes up to 500 events and saves its checkpoint before the next one starts.

**Steps per iteration**

1. Insert a `batch_iteration` row: status `PROCESSING`, `iteration_start_ts`, `from_*` = current checkpoint.
2. Count pending events after the checkpoint → `total_pending`.
3. Record `fetch_start_ts`, fetch up to 500 events ordered by `event_last_modified_ts, event_id` (section 8, query C), record `fetch_end_ts`.
4. If 0 rows fetched: delete or close this iteration row and end the loop (go to step 9).
5. Dedup by `student_id`, keeping the latest event per student.
6. For each student, in order of `event_last_modified_ts`:
   - Found → fetch the record and process / print it.
   - Not found → set `active = false` and log it.
7. Commit the student changes.
8. In ONE transaction, update the iteration row: status `SUCCESS`, `iteration_end_ts`, counts, `remaining`, `to_*` = last event fetched. Then update `batch_scheduler.total_iterations` and `total_processed`.
9. If fewer than 500 rows were fetched, the backlog is drained: set run status `SUCCESS`, `run_end_ts = now`, exit. Otherwise loop to step 1.

**Example: 1,000 pending, batch size 500**

| Iteration | total\_pending | fetched | cumulative\_processed | remaining | status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 1,000 | 500 | 500 | 500 | SUCCESS |
| 2 | 500 | 500 | 1,000 | 0 | SUCCESS |
| 3 | 0 | 0 | 1,000 | 0 | loop ends |

Events that arrive during the run are picked up naturally because `total_pending` is recounted each iteration.

## 6. Use case 3 — Fail

A failure costs at most the one in-flight batch; completed iterations keep their checkpoints.

### 6.1 Handled failure (error is caught)

Examples: network error after JDBC retries are exhausted, SQL error, bad data.

1. Catch the error and stop processing the batch.
2. Roll back uncommitted student changes for this batch.
3. Update the iteration row: status `FAILED`, `iteration_end_ts`, `failed_reason`. Do NOT write `to_*`.
4. Update the run row: status `FAILED`, `failed_ts`, `failed_reason`, `run_end_ts`.
5. Exit. No immediate retry; the next scheduled run resumes.

### 6.2 Hard crash (no chance to update)

Examples: out of memory, process killed, server restart.

- Nothing is written. The iteration row stays `PROCESSING`; the run row stays `PROCESSING` with no `run_end_ts`.
- The next scheduler sees a stuck run. Once it is older than the stale timeout, it marks the run `FAILED` (`STALE_RUN_TIMEOUT`) and starts a new run.

### 6.3 How the next run resumes

The resume point is the `to_*` checkpoint of the latest `SUCCESS` iteration across all runs. `FAILED` and stuck `PROCESSING` iterations are ignored.

**Example: 900k pending, crash after 500k**

- Iterations 1–1,000 succeeded; each saved its checkpoint. Iteration 1,001 crashed.
- Next run reads the checkpoint of iteration 1,000 (the 500,000th event).
- Query C returns only events after that point, so the next run processes the remaining 400k. The 500k done are never read again.
- The 500 events of iteration 1,001 are redone, which is acceptable.

### 6.4 Failure cases covered

| When it fails | Result | Next run |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Before any fetch (startup, DB down) | Run stuck in `INITIATED` or marked `FAILED` | Starts from last checkpoint |
| During event fetch | Iteration `FAILED`, nothing processed | Redoes the same batch |
| During processing (event 300 of 500) | Partial batch rolled back | Redoes the full batch |
| During the success write | Transaction rolls back; iteration not `SUCCESS` | Redoes the batch |
| Hard crash anywhere | Rows left `PROCESSING` | After stale timeout, resumes from last `SUCCESS` iteration |

## 7. Business rules and edge cases

| # | Case | Rule |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | No events pending | Run ends `SUCCESS` with 0 processed |
| 2 | Fewer than 500 events | Process what is there; batch size is a ceiling |
| 3 | Large backlog (e.g. 100k) | Loop batch after batch in the same run until drained |
| 4 | Same student twice in a batch | Dedup; process the latest event only |
| 5 | Student not found | Set `active = false`, log, continue |
| 6 | Same event\_`last_modified_ts` on many events | Tie-break on `event_id`; checkpoint stores both |
| 7 | Events arrive during a run | Picked up in later iterations via recount |
| 8 | Overlapping scheduler trigger | Blocked by status check on latest run |
| 9 | Network blip | JDBC retry layer handles it; if exhausted → handled failure |
| 10 | Reprocessing a batch | Allowed; processing must be idempotent |
| 11 | Order of updates | Always ascending event\_`last_modified_ts, event_id`; never skip ahead |

**Configuration**

- `batch.size` = 500
- `stale.timeout` = e.g. 60 minutes (must exceed the longest normal run)
- `scheduler.cron` = e.g. every 12 hours

## 8. Key SQL queries

**A. Latest run (start check)**

```sql
SELECT run_id, status, run_start_ts
FROM batch_scheduler
ORDER BY run_id DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

**B. Resume checkpoint (latest successful iteration)**

```sql
SELECT to_event_last_modified_ts, to_event_id
FROM batch_iteration
WHERE status = 'SUCCESS'
ORDER BY iteration_id DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

**C. Fetch next batch**

```sql
SELECT event_id, student_id, event_last_modified_ts
FROM student_event
WHERE event_last_modified_ts > :lastTs
   OR (event_last_modified_ts = :lastTs AND event_id > :lastEventId)
ORDER BY event_last_modified_ts, event_id
LIMIT 500;
```

**D. Pending count**

```sql
SELECT COUNT(*)
FROM student_event
WHERE event_last_modified_ts > :lastTs
   OR (event_last_modified_ts = :lastTs AND event_id > :lastEventId);
```

**E. Mark iteration success (same transaction)**

```sql
UPDATE batch_iteration
SET status = 'SUCCESS', iteration_end_ts = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    fetched_count = :fetched, processed_count = :processed,
    cumulative_processed = :cumulative, remaining = :remaining,
    to_event_last_modified_ts = :toTs, to_event_id = :toEventId
WHERE iteration_id = :iterationId;
```

## 9. Open items

- [ ] Confirm `event_last_modified_ts` is stored as UTC and that the JDBC driver and Java code read/bind it as `Instant`/`Timestamp` without converting through local time or a formatted string (avoids clock-skew and time-zone bugs at the checkpoint boundary).
  - [ ] Empty fetch on a run's first iteration: skip inserting an iteration row, mark the run `SUCCESS` directly with `total_processed = 0` and no event range.
  - [ ] Update `batch_scheduler.total_processed` and `event_to_last_modified_ts` in the same transaction as each iteration's `SUCCESS` write, not only at run end, so a crash leaves the run row accurate up to the last committed iteration.
  - [ ] Add an index on `batch_iteration (status, iteration_id)` and on `batch_iteration (run_id)`, plus an index on `batch_scheduler` to find the latest run quickly, so the resume and start-rule queries stay fast as the tables grow.
- [ ] Stale timeout: measure from the latest iteration's `iteration_start_ts`, not `run_start_ts`, so a long 100k catch-up run is not wrongly marked stale.
- [ ] Starting checkpoint for the very first run (all history, or a fixed start date?).
- [ ] Alerting when a run ends `FAILED`, or when no run has succeeded in 24 hours (scheduler itself stopped firing).
- [ ] Retention / purge policy for `batch_scheduler` and `batch_iteration` rows.
